     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "In the hush between cricket songs, when the grass still remembers the warmth of day, a unicorn named Moonberry stepped into the meadow. Her mane fell like quiet rain, and the tip of her horn held a small, steady glow, the way a nightlight does in a dreaming room.\n",
      "\n",
      "Moonberry had a gentle job: she collected fallen wishes. Some dropped from the sky like soft feathers, drifting toward the earth until they tangled in thistles or puddled in spider silk. When she found them, she listened—because wishes, like seeds, open best when someone listens.\n",
      "\n",
      "That evening, she noticed a wish dim as a sigh. It flickered at the edge of the wood, caught in a bramble of what-ifs and could-nots. Moonberry stepped carefully, placing her hooves where the shadows were thickest so she wouldn’t wake the tired daisies. She lowered her head, and the glow at her horn grew warmer, not brighter—like the comfort of a hand held in the dark.\n",
      "\n",
      "“It’s all right,” she whispered, though unicorns use very few words. “Rest first.”\n",
      "\n",
      "At the sound of her quiet, the brambles loosened. The wish uncurled, small as a moth, and nestled against her. Moonberry carried it through the trees, past an owl counting the beats of his own heart, past a brook that hummed lullabies to stones, and out into the open field where the sky leaned low.\n",
      "\n",
      "There, she tipped her horn and sent the little wish upward. It rose, unafraid now, as if it remembered it knew how to shine. When it reached the wide velvet of night, it settled in beside the other stars, and the whole sky seemed to breathe easier.\n",
      "\n",
      "Moonberry watched until the wish steadied. Then she turned home, leaving behind faint hoofprints shaped like pools of moonlight. A fox tucked his tail. The wind smoothed the tall grass. Windows glowed and dimmed, one by one.\n",
      "\n",
      "If you wake and the room is quiet, you might hear the soft thrum of her steps, the kindness that sounds like nothing at all. Close your eyes. Let the crickets keep time. The stars are paying attention, and Moonberry is near—gathering the little lights you thought you’d lost, and putting them back where you can find them in your dreams.\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
